Smatch
======

Smatch is a static analysis tool for C.  It is primarily used to find bugs
in the Linux kernel, but it can also analyze other projects.  Smatch uses a
modified version of Sparse as its C parser.

Building
--------

On Debian or Ubuntu, install the build dependencies with:

    sudo apt-get install gcc make sqlite3 libsqlite3-dev \
        libdbd-sqlite3-perl libssl-dev libtry-tiny-perl

On Fedora, install them with:

    sudo dnf install gcc make sqlite sqlite-devel perl-DBD-SQLite \
        openssl-devel perl-Try-Tiny

Then build Smatch from the source directory:

    make -j$(nproc)

Smatch can be run directly from the build directory; installation is not
required.

Running Smatch
--------------

To check one Linux kernel file, run this command from the kernel source tree:

    ~/path/to/smatch/smatch_scripts/kchecker drivers/example/example.c

The same command accepts a directory:

    ~/path/to/smatch/smatch_scripts/kchecker drivers/example/

For non-kernel projects whose build system supports overriding the compiler,
use ``cgcc`` as the compiler wrapper.  For example:

    make CHECK="~/path/to/smatch/smatch --full-path" \
        CC=~/path/to/smatch/cgcc

See ``Documentation/smatch.rst`` for whole-project analysis, project-specific
options, and the cross-function database.  See
``Documentation/smatch-devel.rst`` for an introduction to Smatch internals and
writing checks.

Source and contact
------------------

The two source repositories are mirrors and contain the same commits:

    https://github.com/error27/smatch
    https://repo.or.cz/w/smatch.git

Send Smatch patches and bug reports to <smatch@vger.kernel.org>.

Smatch is licensed under GPLv2 or later.  The bundled Sparse code is licensed
under the MIT license.  Changes shared with Sparse should be sent to
<linux-sparse@vger.kernel.org>.
